/*! \page page_rest AnCH REST library documentation
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section rest_intro_seq Introduction
 *
 * AnCH REST library declares REST APIs independently of HTTP servers, and provides an HTTP client
 * over native HTTP libraries (see \c anch-rest.todo for the work in progress).
 *
 * \section rest_libs_sec Libraries
 *
 * anch-rest is built by default (option \c ANCH_BUILD_REST , since 0.3). Optional libraries are skipped with a
 * CMake status message when their dependencies are not found.
 *
 * - \c anch-rest-common : HTTP model (\ref anch::rest::Method "Method", \ref anch::rest::Status "Status",
 *   \ref anch::rest::Headers "Headers", \ref anch::rest::MediaType "MediaType", \ref anch::rest::Request "Request",
 *   \ref anch::rest::Response "Response") and codecs (\ref anch::rest::Json "Json" based on anch-json,
 *   \ref anch::rest::Text "Text").
 * - \c anch-rest-server : endpoints declaration and routing (\ref anch::rest::Api "Api").
 * - \c anch-rest-client : HTTP client (\ref anch::rest::Client "Client") over a \ref anch::rest::Transport "Transport".
 * - \c anch-rest-curl : libcurl transport (\ref anch::rest::CurlTransport "CurlTransport"), option \c ANCH_REST_CURL
 *   (built when libcurl is found).
 * - \c anch-rest-nginx : nginx applications bridge (\c ANCH_REST_APPLICATION ), option \c ANCH_REST_NGINX ,
 *   and nginx module sources (\c nginx/ , installed in \c share/anch/nginx-module ).
 * - \c anch-rest-protobuf : Protocol Buffers codecs (\ref anch::rest::Protobuf "Protobuf",
 *   \ref anch::rest::ProtobufJson "ProtobufJson") and RPC services (\ref anch::rest::RpcService "RpcService")
 *   served in an API with Connect and gRPC-Web protocols, Connect client ; option \c ANCH_REST_PROTOBUF (built
 *   when Protobuf is found).
 * - \c anch-rest-grpc : gRPC server (\ref anch::rest::GrpcServer "GrpcServer") for the same RPC services ;
 *   option \c ANCH_REST_GRPC (built when gRPC is found).
 * - \c anch-rest-thrift : Apache Thrift codecs (\ref anch::rest::ThriftBinary "ThriftBinary",
 *   \ref anch::rest::ThriftCompact "ThriftCompact", \ref anch::rest::ThriftJson "ThriftJson"), Thrift services in an
 *   API (\ref anch::rest::installThrift "installThrift") and Thrift transport over the client
 *   (\ref anch::rest::ThriftTransport "ThriftTransport") ; option \c ANCH_REST_THRIFT (built when Thrift and Boost
 *   headers are found).
 *
 * \section rest_server_sec Endpoints declaration
 *
 * Endpoints are declared with a method and a path pattern. Endpoint function arguments are bound from
 * the request: <tt>[const Request&], path parameters..., [Body<T>]</tt>. The result is serialized with the
 * codec negotiated with \c Accept header:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp api
 *
 * Errors: invalid path parameter or body \c 400 , unknown path \c 404 , unknown method \c 405 (with
 * \c Allow ), not acceptable \c 406 , unsupported media type \c 415 , \ref anch::rest::HttpException "HttpException"
 * status, other exceptions \c 500 . \c OPTIONS without endpoint is answered with \c 204 and \c Allow .
 *
 * Media types without codec are read as a stream: a <tt>std::istream&</tt> last argument receives the raw
 * body whatever its \c Content-Type is (absent included, empty stream without body). The client sends a
 * stream with \c Call::body(input, contentType, size) or \c anch::rest::RawBody (streamed by the libcurl
 * transport when the size is known):
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp stream server
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/client.cpp stream client
 *
 * \section rest_form_sec Forms
 *
 * \ref anch::rest::Form "Form" is an ordered list of \ref anch::rest::Part "Part" (fields and files, a
 * name can be repeated). \ref anch::rest::FormUrlEncoded "FormUrlEncoded" codec reads and writes
 * \c application/x-www-form-urlencoded bodies and \ref anch::rest::Multipart "Multipart" codec reads and
 * writes \c multipart/form-data bodies (with files); fields are converted on server side like query
 * parameters:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp form server
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/client.cpp form client
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp multipart server
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/client.cpp multipart client
 *
 * Other types are read and written through a \ref anch::rest::Form "Form" with their
 * \ref anch::rest::FormMapper "FormMapper" (written by hand or generated by anch-oas): helpers convert fields
 * ( \c addFormField , \c formField , \c formFields ), files ( \c addFormFile , \c requiredFormFile ), JSON parts
 * ( \c addJsonFormField , \c jsonFormField ) and check anch-json constraints on server side
 * ( \c checkFormConstraints ):
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp form mapper
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp form mapper api
 *
 * Bodies are limited by side (\ref anch::rest::FormLimits "FormLimits" : body size, number of parts,
 * part size, part headers size): over a limit, server answers \c 413 .
 *
 * Large files do not need memory: multipart parts larger than \c FormLimits::maxMemoryPartSize (1 MiB)
 * are read in temporary files (deleted with their last part copy), parts are written from their file
 * and the client streams multipart bodies ( \c Content-Length is known, libcurl transport reads the
 * stream):
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp multipart limits
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/client.cpp upload file
 * \ref anch::rest::Part::open "Part::open()" reads a part in memory or in a file;
 * \ref anch::rest::Part::content "Part::content()" loads a file in memory.
 *
 * \section rest_cors_sec CORS
 *
 * Cross-Origin Resource Sharing is enabled on the API. Allowed methods are the methods of the routes
 * matching the path. Preflight requests are answered before filters (they have no credentials) and
 * CORS headers are added to every response, errors included:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp cors
 *
 * A preflight with a not allowed origin, method or header is answered with \c 403 without CORS
 * headers. \c Vary: \c Origin is added when origins are listed.
 *
 * \section rest_client_sec Client
 *
 * Path placeholders are replaced in order and percent-encoded. Accepted codecs are part of the call
 * type: \c as<T>() is checked at compile time and deserializes the response with the codec matching
 * its \c Content-Type :
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/client.cpp client
 *
 * Network errors (connection refused, timeout, TLS) are reported with \ref anch::rest::TransportError "TransportError".
 *
 * \section rest_constraints_sec Fields constraints
 *
 * anch-json fields constraints ( \c anch::json::required , \c anch::json::minLength , ...) are checked by the
 * \ref anch::rest::Json "Json" codec according to the side which uses it ( \ref anch::rest::Side "Side" ):
 * - server: request bodies are checked (invalid body: \c 400 response, its body is the error message with the
 *   path of the invalid value, such as <tt>/name: Length 0 is lower than minLength 1</tt>), responses are not ;
 * - client: request bodies are checked before being sent ( \c body<Json>(value) raises
 *   \c anch::json::MappingError , nothing is sent), server responses are trusted and read with tolerance
 *   (unknown fields are ignored, indented responses are accepted).
 *
 * Each side has its own mapping options (server and client can run in the same process), which can be changed at
 * application initialization:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/server.cpp json options
 * Other codecs can behave according to the side with \c serialize(value, out, side) and
 * \c deserialize(value, input, side) functions.
 *
 * \section rest_protobuf_sec Protocol Buffers and gRPC
 *
 * \c protoc messages are read and written by \ref anch::rest::Protobuf "Protobuf" ( \c application/x-protobuf )
 * and \ref anch::rest::ProtobufJson "ProtobufJson" ( \c application/json , Protobuf JSON mapping) codecs:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/protobuf.cpp protobuf codec
 *
 * RPC services are declared once and served with three protocols: Connect and gRPC-Web unary calls in an
 * API (HTTP/1.1: nginx, curl, browsers) and gRPC (HTTP/2) with \ref anch::rest::GrpcServer "GrpcServer":
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/protobuf.cpp rpc service
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/grpc.cpp grpc server
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/protobuf.cpp rpc client
 *
 * Handler \ref anch::rest::HttpException "HttpException" status is converted ( \c 400 is \c INVALID_ARGUMENT ,
 * \c 404 is \c NOT_FOUND , ...), other exceptions are \c INTERNAL without message. Request and response
 * metadata (HTTP headers, gRPC metadata) are given by \ref anch::rest::RpcContext "RpcContext". Native gRPC
 * clients use \c grpc_cpp_plugin stubs. Only unary calls are supported.
 *
 * \section rest_thrift_sec Apache Thrift
 *
 * Structures generated by <tt>thrift --gen cpp</tt> are read and written by
 * \ref anch::rest::ThriftBinary "ThriftBinary" ( \c application/vnd.apache.thrift.binary ),
 * \ref anch::rest::ThriftCompact "ThriftCompact" ( \c .compact ) and \ref anch::rest::ThriftJson "ThriftJson"
 * ( \c .json ) codecs. Thrift services are served over HTTP by an API, as Thrift \c THttpServer (the protocol
 * is chosen with the \c Content-Type , \c application/x-thrift is the binary protocol), and generated clients
 * call them through an anch-rest client:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/thrift.cpp thrift rpc
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/thrift.cpp thrift client
 *
 * \section rest_nginx_sec nginx module
 *
 * An application is a shared library which declares its API factory:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-rest/examples/nginxApp.cpp nginx application
 *
 * The module is built with nginx (\c --with-threads to execute handlers in a thread pool):
 * \code{.txt}
./configure --with-threads --with-compat --add-dynamic-module=<anch-rest/nginx>
make modules
\endcode
 *
 * nginx configuration:
 * \code{.txt}
load_module modules/ngx_http_anch_rest_module.so;
thread_pool default threads=16;
http {
  server {
    location /api/ {
      anch_rest_app /usr/lib/libmyapi.so "/etc/myapi.conf";
      anch_rest_thread_pool default;   # default value; "off" executes handlers in the event loop
      anch_rest_buffers 4 16k;         # default value: response buffers of a request
    }
  }
}
\endcode
 *
 * Libraries are loaded when configuration is read (\c nginx -t reports loading errors) and APIs are
 * created in each worker process.
 *
 * Request and response bodies are streamed (\c anch::cutils::CIStream and \c anch::cutils::COStream on the
 * module C functions):
 * - in a thread pool, the handler is started as soon as request headers have been read. It reads the request
 *   body while nginx receives it (unbuffered body: at most \c client_body_buffer_size bytes are waiting for the
 *   handler), and its response body is sent while it is written, with at most \c anch_rest_buffers in memory: the
 *   handler waits while they are being sent ;
 * - the response is started (status and headers) when more than one buffer has been written, with chunked
 *   transfer encoding, or at the end of the body with its \c Content-Length when it fits in one buffer. An error
 *   raised before the start is answered with \c 500 , an error raised after closes the connection (truncated
 *   response) ;
 * - an application can stream a large response with \ref anch::rest::Response::stream "Response::stream" ; the
 *   request outlives the response body writer, which can read the request body ;
 * - a slow client keeps a thread pool thread busy while it sends its request body, or receives the end of a
 *   response larger than buffers: thread pool size, \c client_body_timeout and \c send_timeout bound it ;
 * - without thread pool (\c anch_rest_thread_pool \c off ), the handler is executed in the event loop: the request
 *   body is buffered by nginx (memory, then temporary file) and read in blocks, and the response body is given to
 *   nginx as it is written, without flow control (a large response stays in memory until it has been sent).
 */
